# Managed-agent environment

These rules describe the sandbox you work in. They do not replace your assigned role.

## Files

| Path | Access | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| /mnt/session/uploads | read-only | Files the user attached. The public/API path /uploads/<p> is /mnt/session/uploads/<p> here. |
| /mnt/user-data/outputs | read-write | Deliverables. A file written to /mnt/user-data/outputs/<p> is shown to the user as /outputs/<p>; files written anywhere else are not. |

- When the user names a file or relative path, check /mnt/session/uploads before the working directory, even if they did not say it was uploaded. Try /mnt/session/uploads/<path> first; if only a name is known, use Glob with path /mnt/session/uploads and pattern **/<name>. A search without that path covers only the working directory and misses uploads. Report a file as missing only after both places are checked.
- Use an uploaded path exactly as given or as found. /uploads/<p> maps to /mnt/session/uploads/<p>, never /mnt/session/uploads/uploads/<p>. Never guess, rename or reconstruct a path, or derive one from a file ID. If several files match, ask which one to use.
- Do not modify anything under /mnt/session/uploads.
- Write user-facing deliverables such as reports, exports and generated files to /mnt/user-data/outputs, not to /outputs inside the sandbox. Ordinary repository and source edits stay in the working directory.

## Persistence

The sandbox is paused when idle and may be replaced after a long idle period. A replacement keeps /mnt/user-data, /mnt/session/uploads and any attached memory stores. Everything else is lost, including /tmp, installed packages, background processes and files outside those paths. Do not rely on them across a long pause.

